      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AWS EC2\n(Elastic compute cloud)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "DJjlE_su7NlH9hLd0WJWR",
      "originalText": "AWS EC2\n(Elastic compute cloud)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ebjDJP1guLfGeExg_V36V",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1903.8996862289837,
      "y": 4622.164299823278,
      "width": 1221,
      "height": 650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGa",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1431732180,
      "version": 1386,
      "versionNonce": 1115931735,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lets say our frontend, backend all things is ready, next thing is we need to go live, so what we \ncan do\n\n1. Buy a new machine(Actual hardware)\n2. Setup the OS\n3. Setup our project\n4. Make site go live\n\n\nfirstly this machine is able to handle the load, but after some time this is not able to, so we will\nagain go to market and we will buy one more capable new machines.\nIf someone does these two tasks for us, 1 -> buying a new machine, 2 -> setup OS in it, so that we can\njust go to portal and buy a machine, that will be superfast process, that in a nut shell AWS EC2 does\n\n\nCompute -> the resource that we are actually borrowing from AWS, this resource is a virtual computing Engine,\nmeans its kind of hardware that can do computations. so this service EC2 help us to buy a new computer\nfrom AWS.\n\nElastic -> it actually represents flexibility, if any time we want 5 machine, we can immidiately get 5 machines\nwith exact same configuration. we can also drop out whenever needed\n\nSo now we just need to rent a machine , setup project and go live.\n\nEC2 is a computing capacity(Virtual Computer Only) that is scalable in AWS.  Using this we dont need to invest\nin actual hardware upfront.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lets say our frontend, backend all things is ready, next thing is we need to go live, so what we \ncan do\n\n1. Buy a new machine(Actual hardware)\n2. Setup the OS\n3. Setup our project\n4. Make site go live\n\n\nfirstly this machine is able to handle the load, but after some time this is not able to, so we will\nagain go to market and we will buy one more capable new machines.\nIf someone does these two tasks for us, 1 -> buying a new machine, 2 -> setup OS in it, so that we can\njust go to portal and buy a machine, that will be superfast process, that in a nut shell AWS EC2 does\n\n\nCompute -> the resource that we are actually borrowing from AWS, this resource is a virtual computing Engine,\nmeans its kind of hardware that can do computations. so this service EC2 help us to buy a new computer\nfrom AWS.\n\nElastic -> it actually represents flexibility, if any time we want 5 machine, we can immidiately get 5 machines\nwith exact same configuration. we can also drop out whenever needed\n\nSo now we just need to rent a machine , setup project and go live.\n\nEC2 is a computing capacity(Virtual Computer Only) that is scalable in AWS.  Using this we dont need to invest\nin actual hardware upfront.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "QWpTl_fhlkyQpVExCSb4E",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1905.0598424789837,
      "y": 5333.394768573278,
      "width": 242,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1254813292,
      "version": 28,
      "versionNonce": 1808437401,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Features of AWS EC2 ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Features of AWS EC2 ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ODpzAl3pbjQ6hLmmmbe0l",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1950.8098424789837,
      "y": 5376.769768573278,
      "width": 1045,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGc",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1206579692,
      "version": 319,
      "versionNonce": 2018976119,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Provides virtual computing resource.\n2. Computers available are of diff diff configurations(CPU, Storage, RAM etc are configurable).\n3. Provide secure access to the machines(key pair based)\n4. There are pre configured AMI(Amazon machine images) available.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Provides virtual computing resource.\n2. Computers available are of diff diff configurations(CPU, Storage, RAM etc are configurable).\n3. Provide secure access to the machines(key pair based)\n4. There are pre configured AMI(Amazon machine images) available.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FSfEm7vXQmylRExQ7VKFx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1903.3020299789837,
      "y": 5576.515862323278,
      "width": 242,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1917158124,
      "version": 34,
      "versionNonce": 893326713,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "EC2 Pricing Options ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "EC2 Pricing Options ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "9LJyjyonKmYR81omHnv5N",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1945.5832799789837,
      "y": 5614.648674823278,
      "width": 1133,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGe",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1549471188,
      "version": 1058,
      "versionNonce": 302111383,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "we can work on Free tier.\n\nit provides two ways of pricing\n1. On demand -> Here we dont have to pay always a fixed price, it will be dependent on howmuch \n    traffic our EC2 is actually handling.It also depend on what kind of instance we are having\n    suppose we are having a linux instance, then they are going to charge us on second basis, If\nwe are having a windonw instance, then they are going to charge us by the hour based on the usage.\nIf some sesons we have very very low traffic, then this is beneficial.\nIt is also beneficial if we want to move on other cloud later.\n\n\n2. Reserved -> Here there is a contract that happen between us and Amazon, this contract can be 1 year,\n    2 year, it tells us we have to pay this money upfront and then we have this much of bandwidth of\n    each and every resource. prepaid model.\n    If always we are on high traffic, or we know this is the traffic always.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "we can work on Free tier.\n\nit provides two ways of pricing\n1. On demand -> Here we dont have to pay always a fixed price, it will be dependent on howmuch \n    traffic our EC2 is actually handling.It also depend on what kind of instance we are having\n    suppose we are having a linux instance, then they are going to charge us on second basis, If\nwe are having a windonw instance, then they are going to charge us by the hour based on the usage.\nIf some sesons we have very very low traffic, then this is beneficial.\nIt is also beneficial if we want to move on other cloud later.\n\n\n2. Reserved -> Here there is a contract that happen between us and Amazon, this contract can be 1 year,\n    2 year, it tells us we have to pay this money upfront and then we have this much of bandwidth of\n    each and every resource. prepaid model.\n    If always we are on high traffic, or we know this is the traffic always.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RJ8KhNOw4bamtRH3k2nXm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1945.0598424789837,
      "y": 6109.394768573278,
      "width": 44,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGg",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1786181100,
      "version": 10,
      "versionNonce": 1804598873,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SSH ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SSH ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "sPHNu4LYOYWgDhmSDhDc1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1971.3137487289837,
      "y": 6140.894768573278,
      "width": 968,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGh",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1720265836,
      "version": 398,
      "versionNonce": 1412984759,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "It help us remote login securely. means we can login to our machine without accessing it\nphysically. what it needs, it should connected with internet and machine should have IP \naddress.\n\nusing internet, key value pair, IP address we can login in our machihe using ssh. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "It help us remote login securely. means we can login to our machine without accessing it\nphysically. what it needs, it should connected with internet and machine should have IP \naddress.\n\nusing internet, key value pair, IP address we can login in our machihe using ssh. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "e8yN67Cp8OL-_Ap8_s_R6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1949.7045671076967,
      "y": 6359.130825968081,
      "width": 1144,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGi",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1927921190,
      "version": 361,
      "versionNonce": 2106641209,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Security Group -> It is set of configurations, that tells us what type of network request are allowed on\n    the instance that we are borrowing.\n\n    Allow SSH -> means anybody in the internet can do ssh connection with our machine, not just internet\n        they also need to have the secret pem key in order to access our machine.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Security Group -> It is set of configurations, that tells us what type of network request are allowed on\n    the instance that we are borrowing.\n\n    Allow SSH -> means anybody in the internet can do ssh connection with our machine, not just internet\n        they also need to have the secret pem key in order to access our machine.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "h-pE8P00dj466lrWoRvUx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1941.7944108576967,
      "y": 6634.638638468081,
      "width": 1309,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1343827450,
      "version": 2002,
      "versionNonce": 2032099543,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Connecting to EC2 using SSH -> we are interested in to know public ip address, because if we want to \n       connect via internet to any particular machine on the internet we need to know its public ipv4\n        address or maybe the public ipv6 address.\n\n    Now we will come to Command line interface of our computer, becuase ssh protocol works from the\n    command line interface all together.\n\n    First thing that we are going to do is , we are going to change permissions of our key.\n    \n    Why we need to change the permission of key? ->\n        permissioin of key need to be change before we start using it, so that nobody else can hamper this\n        particular key.\n\n        In Linux/MAC if we want to change the permission of any key, we can use chmod, it changes read, write\n            execute permission for the key.\n\n        chmod 400 myserver.pem -> 400 means we only give read access, no write access, no executing access\n        \n        ls -lh -> we can see the permission of key using this\n        chomod 700 myserver.pem -> it means current user has access of read, write and execute everything\n\n        ssh -i myserver.pem username@ip(ubuntu@publicIpv4) -> -i to actually show, we are going to use a public key of \n                server to login.\n\n        whoami -> ubuntu -> we are inside rented EC2 machine, with OS ubuntu\n\n        first thing that we should always do with the linux machine that we have borrowed is\n        sudo apt-get update -> this is going to update our rented Linux machine, all the default linux packages\n                and everything it is going to update, because internet on these data center is super fast, \n                most of these commands that we are going to run here is superfast",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Connecting to EC2 using SSH -> we are interested in to know public ip address, because if we want to \n       connect via internet to any particular machine on the internet we need to know its public ipv4\n        address or maybe the public ipv6 address.\n\n    Now we will come to Command line interface of our computer, becuase ssh protocol works from the\n    command line interface all together.\n\n    First thing that we are going to do is , we are going to change permissions of our key.\n    \n    Why we need to change the permission of key? ->\n        permissioin of key need to be change before we start using it, so that nobody else can hamper this\n        particular key.\n\n        In Linux/MAC if we want to change the permission of any key, we can use chmod, it changes read, write\n            execute permission for the key.\n\n        chmod 400 myserver.pem -> 400 means we only give read access, no write access, no executing access\n        \n        ls -lh -> we can see the permission of key using this\n        chomod 700 myserver.pem -> it means current user has access of read, write and execute everything\n\n        ssh -i myserver.pem username@ip(ubuntu@publicIpv4) -> -i to actually show, we are going to use a public key of \n                server to login.\n\n        whoami -> ubuntu -> we are inside rented EC2 machine, with OS ubuntu\n\n        first thing that we should always do with the linux machine that we have borrowed is\n        sudo apt-get update -> this is going to update our rented Linux machine, all the default linux packages\n                and everything it is going to update, because internet on these data center is super fast, \n                most of these commands that we are going to run here is superfast",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "T_-wYHyQW729iD9Tmn8MJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1962.7436296076967,
      "y": 7498.693325968081,
      "width": 385,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1086661926,
      "version": 87,
      "versionNonce": 866450457,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Some more deeper concepts on EC2 ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Some more deeper concepts on EC2 ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "w8rdkCOUCkDv4-ZZLEe-P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2017.8256608576967,
      "y": 7569.400357218081,
      "width": 1100,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGm",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2138235686,
      "version": 1468,
      "versionNonce": 38481399,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AMI(Amazon Machine Image) -> It is a pre configured image provided by AWS that contains the\n    necessary information required to launch an instance.\n    when we say the word image, inside an image we actually configure every single detail that needs\n    to be installed for the operating system, and same reference of image is present here also.\n\n    It is kind of like a template that contains the software configurations(OS, Application servers\n    , application) with which we boot(launch) a new machine. \n    This contains information on OS, applications to be installed and more.\n\n    Using this we are telling what OS we want to install in our EC2 instance.\n    \n    We can create our own AMI also.\n\n    As we discusse in AMI, we dont just have the OS level details but we also have what all \n    application we want to install, What we can do is, we can setup our own AMI configurations\n    we can take a base AMI like a base ubuntu AMI, and then on top of if we write some code may\n    be a shell script, using which we can define what all more software we want to install\n\n    Like we want to launch an EC2 instance using some custom AMI, this will not only setup ubuntu it\n    will also setup project for us. This is we can do with AMI.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "AMI(Amazon Machine Image) -> It is a pre configured image provided by AWS that contains the\n    necessary information required to launch an instance.\n    when we say the word image, inside an image we actually configure every single detail that needs\n    to be installed for the operating system, and same reference of image is present here also.\n\n    It is kind of like a template that contains the software configurations(OS, Application servers\n    , application) with which we boot(launch) a new machine. \n    This contains information on OS, applications to be installed and more.\n\n    Using this we are telling what OS we want to install in our EC2 instance.\n    \n    We can create our own AMI also.\n\n    As we discusse in AMI, we dont just have the OS level details but we also have what all \n    application we want to install, What we can do is, we can setup our own AMI configurations\n    we can take a base AMI like a base ubuntu AMI, and then on top of if we write some code may\n    be a shell script, using which we can define what all more software we want to install\n\n    Like we want to launch an EC2 instance using some custom AMI, this will not only setup ubuntu it\n    will also setup project for us. This is we can do with AMI.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "lmiu0aYmwpu1YEo-qG-1F",
