      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Elastic Network Interface",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "x4MtRkhOBwfWKh2spxi7D",
      "originalText": "Elastic Network Interface",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "AEV_GGCcUyqkxHh9wlg-J",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1923.3803483576967,
      "y": 9919.78902909308,
      "width": 1265,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHf",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1377292872,
      "version": 1759,
      "versionNonce": 1554956569,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Whenever communication between different network elements has to happen, they has to happen like around\nsome IP address and some communication protocol.\n\nWhat ENI going to do  -> It going to act as a Network interface, act like a virtual network card that is\n    going to be attached to our EC2 instance, because in our machine as well we have a network card, we can\nassume it like a virtual network card. SO it acts as like a virtual network card for EC2 instance.\n\nUsing this elastic network interface our EC2 instances will be able to communicate with other machines\nacross the internet, this ENI is kind of like a logical networking component that can be used to provide\nIP address and attach it to diff subnets.\n\nWe need to configure this whole network interface also in order to make sure that we are able to communicate\nfrom outside to our EC2 instance, how we will be able to do this?\n\nGo To EC2 instance -> security -> security groups\n\nSecurity groups are use to secure EC2 instances from any unwanted request, we dont want to be in a situation\nwhere anybody in the internet just able to directly call to our EC2 instance, we need to configure our security\ngroup to secure our EC2. If we will not attach any security group then a default security group will attach to\nEC2 instance.\n\nSecurity Group is nothing but a set of configuration which tells that what all ports and what kind of communication\nwe are allowing or not allowing on our EC2.\n\nFrom any machine there can be two kind of communication",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Whenever communication between different network elements has to happen, they has to happen like around\nsome IP address and some communication protocol.\n\nWhat ENI going to do  -> It going to act as a Network interface, act like a virtual network card that is\n    going to be attached to our EC2 instance, because in our machine as well we have a network card, we can\nassume it like a virtual network card. SO it acts as like a virtual network card for EC2 instance.\n\nUsing this elastic network interface our EC2 instances will be able to communicate with other machines\nacross the internet, this ENI is kind of like a logical networking component that can be used to provide\nIP address and attach it to diff subnets.\n\nWe need to configure this whole network interface also in order to make sure that we are able to communicate\nfrom outside to our EC2 instance, how we will be able to do this?\n\nGo To EC2 instance -> security -> security groups\n\nSecurity groups are use to secure EC2 instances from any unwanted request, we dont want to be in a situation\nwhere anybody in the internet just able to directly call to our EC2 instance, we need to configure our security\ngroup to secure our EC2. If we will not attach any security group then a default security group will attach to\nEC2 instance.\n\nSecurity Group is nothing but a set of configuration which tells that what all ports and what kind of communication\nwe are allowing or not allowing on our EC2.\n\nFrom any machine there can be two kind of communication",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
