      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Outbound or outgoing",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "dFz0W85IpClVPixkZdl9T",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2740.9936296076967,
      "y": 10747.64840409308,
      "width": 77,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHn",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1297490488,
      "version": 12,
      "versionNonce": 1118629017,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "request",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "request",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "y6Z-gP1QKMkdcZV0SAcVd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1914.7319108576967,
      "y": 10902.43746659308,
      "width": 1254,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHo",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 710835512,
      "version": 1944,
      "versionNonce": 1012434295,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For example lets say we have a web server and there is a react frontend, this frontend web\nbrowser will be making a request to our web server, that will be incoming request to our server\nor machine on which that server is deployed, if server is going to download some thing from any\nother server then thats a outgoing request, in this way security group classify the request, inbound\nand outbound\n\nOutbound rules -> here we will define what kind of request can go from EC2 machine to any other.\n    Type -> All traffic\n    Protocol -> All\n    Port Range -> All\n    it means we can make any type of request from our EC2 machine to the outside one.\n\nInbound Rules -> it defines that for outside world what all communication protocol are available\n    SSH -> port 22\n           source -> custom 0.0.0.0/0 -> means anyone can communicate to our machine using port 22 or ssh protocol\n     \n    HTTP -> port 80\n        source -> custom 0.0.0.0/0 -> means anyone can communicate to our machine using port 80 or HTTP protocol\n    \n    Custom TCP -> port 3000\n        source -> custom 0.0.0.0/0 -> means anayone can communicate to our machine using port 3000 using internet \n        we cn also give our IP or any particular machine IP, from whihc we want to accept communication in source\n\n\nssh -i pemkey ubuntu@publicip -> now we are inside EC2\nlets say we have run our server in port 3000 and then close the terminal then ssh connection will also\n    be lost, to make connection up we need to run this in background\n\nps aux -> it will give the process id  in which our server is running\nkill pid -> it will kill the process",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For example lets say we have a web server and there is a react frontend, this frontend web\nbrowser will be making a request to our web server, that will be incoming request to our server\nor machine on which that server is deployed, if server is going to download some thing from any\nother server then thats a outgoing request, in this way security group classify the request, inbound\nand outbound\n\nOutbound rules -> here we will define what kind of request can go from EC2 machine to any other.\n    Type -> All traffic\n    Protocol -> All\n    Port Range -> All\n    it means we can make any type of request from our EC2 machine to the outside one.\n\nInbound Rules -> it defines that for outside world what all communication protocol are available\n    SSH -> port 22\n           source -> custom 0.0.0.0/0 -> means anyone can communicate to our machine using port 22 or ssh protocol\n     \n    HTTP -> port 80\n        source -> custom 0.0.0.0/0 -> means anyone can communicate to our machine using port 80 or HTTP protocol\n    \n    Custom TCP -> port 3000\n        source -> custom 0.0.0.0/0 -> means anayone can communicate to our machine using port 3000 using internet \n        we cn also give our IP or any particular machine IP, from whihc we want to accept communication in source\n\n\nssh -i pemkey ubuntu@publicip -> now we are inside EC2\nlets say we have run our server in port 3000 and then close the terminal then ssh connection will also\n    be lost, to make connection up we need to run this in background\n\nps aux -> it will give the process id  in which our server is running\nkill pid -> it will kill the process",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XNAaYyYWBmouisy-HsWYZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1912.7319108576967,
      "y": 11703.54293534308,
      "width": 627,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHq",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2077103800,
      "version": 69,
      "versionNonce": 930476409,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How Can we convert something into a Background Process ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How Can we convert something into a Background Process ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Cngo27M8vEFnhI5_pnD6p",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1969.3881608576967,
      "y": 11740.18746659308,
      "width": 1067,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHs",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 536312264,
      "version": 481,
      "versionNonce": 406240919,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785418186027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Background process are also known as Daemon Process , Daemon Process are the process that run in \nbackground, that doesn't run in foreground\n\nEvery language has some kind of package, for nodejs we have pm2.\nnpm i pm2 -g -> install globally in the machine\n\nusing this pm2 we are able to run our server as a daemon process.\n\npm2 start \"python3 manage.py runserver\" -> change pm2 with python package",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Background process are also known as Daemon Process , Daemon Process are the process that run in \nbackground, that doesn't run in foreground\n\nEvery language has some kind of package, for nodejs we have pm2.\nnpm i pm2 -g -> install globally in the machine\n\nusing this pm2 we are able to run our server as a daemon process.\n\npm2 start \"python3 manage.py runserver\" -> change pm2 with python package",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "TlzAoiJX8if5VNJ35KjZR",
